Flow of MVC Architecture:
Client → DispatcherServlet → Controller → Service → Repository → DB → Response

DispatcherServlet:
the front controller 
it receives all incoming HTTP requests, decides where to send them, and sends back the response.
DispatcherServlet acts as the entry point for every request.
It follows the Front Controller Pattern.
One servlet → handles all requests → delegates to the correct controller → builds response.

Flow:
Browser
  ↓
DispatcherServlet (Front Controller)
  ↓
HandlerMapping → Finds which Controller should handle "/employees/101"
  ↓
Controller (e.g. EmployeeController)
  ↓
Service Layer (Business logic)
  ↓
Repository Layer (Database call)
  ↓
Controller returns Model/Data
  ↓
ViewResolver (for web pages) or JSON converter (for REST)
  ↓
DispatcherServlet → sends final response to browser


| Layer          | Responsibility                 |
| -------------- | ------------------------------ |
| **Controller** | Handles incoming HTTP requests |
| **Service**    | Business logic                 |
| **Repository** | Data access (JPA)              |

Boot automatically registers DispatcherServlet mapped to /


| Annotation                                                     | Purpose                        |
| -------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `@Controller`                                                  | Returns a **view** (HTML)      |
| `@RestController`                                              | Returns **JSON**/Response body |
| `@RequestMapping`                                              | Base path for controller       |
| `@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@DeleteMapping` | Define HTTP methods            |


